# The constructors Interval(Expansion<T, 2>, Expansion<T, 2>), Interval(Expansion<T, 2>) and
# from_bounds of two numbers of two limbs (milestone2.md, "Interval kernels", "Values of an
# interval"): the bounds are Expansion<T, 2> objects whose limbs the program may have written, so
# each limb passes through guard (rule R3), as the center given to a ball of two limbs does
# (ball_make_2_entry: the owner's decision of 2026-11-23, one rule for every constructor; that this
# constructor is closed the same way is a choice of the authors); then the listing of
# interval_make_2 on the guarded limbs, written out, not called, so that the printer puts its
# selects behind a branch as for interval_make_2 (cpp.branch takes a kernel's own selects). Its
# claims are those of interval_make_2, whose statement is about values: the guard is the identity
# on a canonical pair as a value (a zero limb made +0, an infinity or a NaN staying what it is).
# Four additions more than interval_make_2, which compares and selects only.
kernel:        interval_make_2_entry
input:         supplied lo = lo0 lo1
input:         supplied hi = hi0 hi1
output:        zl = zl0 zl1
output:        zu = zu0 zu1
requires:      canonical lo
requires:      canonical hi
ensures:       makes_interval zl zu from=lo,hi
min_precision: 1
source:        interval_make_2 on the guarded limbs of the bounds (rule R3, as ball_make_2_entry)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeMakeEntry.v interval_make_2_entry_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   every output is selected after explicit tests of the bounds; makes_interval states what comes out

gl0 = guard(lo0)
gl1 = guard(lo1)
gu0 = guard(hi0)
gu1 = guard(hi1)
ok  = le_2_2(gl0, gl1, gu0, gu1)
nl  = lt(gl0, const inf)
mi  = neg(const inf)
nu  = lt(mi, gu0)
va  = and(ok, nl)
v   = and(va, nu)
rv  = lt_2_2(gu0, gu1, gl0, gl1)
el  = select(rv, const inf, mi)
eu  = select(rv, mi, const inf)
zl0 = select(v, gl0, el)
zl1 = select(v, gl1, 0)
zu0 = select(v, gu0, eu)
zu1 = select(v, gu1, 0)
